course: dictionary
lesson: 12256
title: Consumption: using things up
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Consumption is a noun, and it comes from the verb «consume», which means to use up. Its meaning depends on context. In science, it means how much of something is used up. In everyday talk, it can mean eating or drinking, or buying and using things.
explain: «The car has good fuel consumption, so it uses very little petrol.» Here consumption means how much fuel is used up. Try the swap check: "The car has good fuel use, so it uses very little petrol." It still makes sense, so you have the right meaning.
explain: «This water is not fit for human consumption.» This is a formal way of saying people should not drink or eat it. The notice is not talking about a bill or a shop. The words fit for and human show you that it is about what people can safely use.
explain: In old stories you may meet, «She died of consumption.» This is an old name for tuberculosis, a lung disease. Today we rarely use the word this way, so when you see it in an old book, check the clues and a dictionary.
fact: Consumption is a noun that means the amount of something that is used up.
fact: «Fuel consumption» means how much fuel a vehicle uses, such as litres per 100 km.
fact: «Consumption» can also mean the act of eating, drinking or using something, as in «not fit for human consumption».
fact: In business, consumption means buying and using goods and services, as in «consumer spending».
fact: «Consumption» is also an old name for tuberculosis (TB), a lung disease, and you will mostly find it in old stories.
exercise: «The car has low fuel consumption, so it uses very little petrol.» What does fuel consumption mean? | The tank where the fuel is kept | *The amount of fuel that is used up | The price of fuel at the petrol station
exercise: The sign says, «Not fit for human consumption.» What does this mean? | It is good food for people to eat | It is only for sale to shops | *It is not safe for people to eat or drink
exercise: In «Shoppers' consumption of goods rises at Christmas», what does consumption mean? | *Buying and using goods | Cooking food on a stove | Stopping things from working | Old, broken things
test: «Our class tracks our water consumption each week.» What does the class measure? | How cold the water is | *How much water we use | How much water we drink in one sip | How clean the water tastes
test: In an old story, «She died of consumption.» Consumption here is an old name for what? | A bad cold from the weather | Being very hungry | *Tuberculosis, a lung disease
exam: «The car uses 6 litres of fuel for every 100 km.» In this maths context, what does the fuel consumption describe? | *How much fuel is used up over a distance | The number of cars on the road | The price of a litre of petrol | The speed the car can reach
